  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLow pressure will slowly shift east across the Midwest through\nSunday, bringing multiple rounds of rain to the local area for\nthe rest of the weekend. Any precipitation this afternoon into\nthe start of this evening will be light, although rainfall rates\nwill likely pick up as better moisture lifts into the area\ntonight. A few thunderstorms can't be ruled out, but severe \nweather is not anticipated tonight. By early Sunday morning, \nmost spots across the area will see at least a quarter of an \ninch of rain with locations along and west of I-75 likely \nreceiving up to 0.75\" of rain. There are some signals for \ntraining and locally higher amounts of 1-2\" along the far \nwestern fringe of the local area, although that higher \nprecipitation axis may be to the west of the CWA. Will need to \nkeep an eye on where swaths of heavier rain occur, but in \ngeneral this initial round of rain will largely serve as a \nprimer for heavy rainfall on Sunday.\n\nA break in precipitation (or at least, lower coverage in shower\nactivity) is likely Sunday morning, especially across north- \ncentral Ohio. By the afternoon, better upper level support will \nmove over the region, allowing additional showers and \nthunderstorms to develop and move east through the rest of the \nafternoon and into the evening. This will be the window for the \nheaviest rainfall and likely the greatest flash flooding risk. \nMost of the area will receive 0.5 to 0.75\" of additional \nrainfall Sunday afternoon/evening with some areas possibly \nreceiving 2 or more inches of rainfall where showers and \nthunderstorms train. Latest guidance suggests that the highest \nrain amounts could fall across portions of NE OH/NW PA which \nwould result in an enhanced flash flooding risk, but will need \nto see how high res models trend over the next 12-18 hours. Have\nopted against issuing a Flood Watch with this update since \nconfidence in the placement of the heaviest rainfall/greatest \ntraining potential is still medium at best. Would like to see \nwhere the heaviest rain occurs tonight before making a final \ndecision on any headlines. On Sunday, there is a Slight Risk \n(Level 2 of 4) of excessive rainfall/flash flooding across the \nmajority of the local area.\n\nThere is still potential for isolated strong to severe\nthunderstorms Sunday afternoon into Sunday evening. Jet support\nwill increase Sunday afternoon as the low meanders eastward into\nthe Ohio Valley, resulting in increased shear. There's still \nsome uncertainty with how much we destabilize given the \npotential for morning showers/thunderstorms. If we manage to \nclear out between rounds of rain then instability will be \nhigher, but any lingering showers/clouds could result in lower \ninstability and a lower severe weather threat. Based on the \nshear profile/wind field, any thunderstorms could produce \ndamaging wind gusts and possibly a brief tornado Sunday \nafternoon/evening. There is a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) of \nsevere weather.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe low will finally lift northeast of the area on Monday,\nalthough persistent upper troughing will result continued\nchances of rain showers through much of Monday. A brief period\nof dry weather is anticipated on Tuesday before rain chances\nincrease as a shortwave lifts into the region Wednesday.\nScattered rain chances continue through the end of the week as\nan upper trough shifts east into the area Thursday/Friday.\nTemperatures will generally be near normal with highs in the 80s\nand lows in the 60s anticipated.",
